'''''Musa'' × ''paradisiaca''''' is a [[species]] as well as a [[cultivar]], originating as the [[hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] between ''[[Musa acuminata]]'' and ''[[Musa balbisiana]]'', cultivated and domesticated by humans very early. Most cultivated [[banana]]s and [[Plantain (cooking)|plantain]]s are [[polyploid]] [[cultivar]]s either of this hybrid or of ''M.&nbsp;acuminata'' alone. [[Carl Linnaeus|Linnaeus]] originally used the name ''M.&nbsp;paradisiaca'' only for plantains or cooking bananas, but the modern usage includes hybrid cultivars used both for cooking and as dessert bananas. Linnaeus's name for dessert bananas, ''Musa sapientum'', is thus a [[synonym (taxonomy)|synonym]] of ''Musa'' × ''paradisiaca''.

==Origin==
{{main|Banana|Cooking plantain}}

Almost all cultivated plantains and many cultivated bananas are [[cultivar]]s of the [[hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] between two wild species, ''M. acuminata'' and ''M. balbisiana''. It is believed that [[Southeast Asian]] farmers first [[domestication|domesticated]] ''M.&nbsp;acuminata''. When the cultivated plants spread north-west into areas where ''M.balbisiana'' was native (see map), hybrids between the two species occurred and were then developed further into a wide range of cultivars.<ref name=Ploe07/>

Hundreds of cultivars of two species are known, possessing characteristics that are highly variable, but broadly intermediate between the ancestral species. They are typically {{convert|2|-|9|m|ft|0}} tall when mature. The above-ground part of the plant is a "false stem" or [[pseudostem]], consisting of leaves and their fused bases. Each pseudostem can produce a single flowering stem. After fruiting, the pseudostem dies, but offshoots may develop from the base of the plant. Cultivars of banana are usually sterile, without seeds or viable pollen.<ref name=Nels06/>

==Taxonomy==
{{main|Banana#Taxonomy|List of banana cultivars#Classification|l1=Banana – Taxonomy|l2=List of banana cultivars – Classification}}

[[Banana]] plants were originally classified by [[Carl Linnaeus|Linnaeus]] into two species, which he called ''Musa paradisiaca'' for those used as cooking bananas ([[Plantain (cooking)|plantain]]s), and ''M. sapientum'' for those used as dessert bananas. It was later discovered that both of his "species" were actually cultivated varieties of the hybrid between two wild species, ''[[Musa acuminata|M. acuminata]]'' and ''[[Musa balbisiana|M. balbisiana]]'', which is now called ''M.'' × ''paradisiaca'' L.<ref name=Valm00/> The [[circumscription (taxonomy)|circumscription]] of the modern [[taxon]] ''M.'' × ''paradisiaca'' thus includes both the original ''M. paradisiaca'' and ''M. sapientum'', the latter being reduced to a [[synonym (taxonomy)|synonym]] of ''M.''&nbsp;×&nbsp;''paradisiaca''.<ref name=WCSP_254888/>

In pre-Linnean times this banana was named <nowiki>'</nowiki>''Musa serapionis''<nowiki>'</nowiki>, for instance by [[Maria Sybilla Merian]] in her book, ''[[Metamorphosis insectorum Surinamensium]],'' in 1705.<ref name=serapionis>{{cite book|last=Merian|first=Maria Sybilla|author-link=Maria Sybilla Merian|date=1705|title=Metamorphosis insectorum Surinamensium|url=https://archive.org/details/Metamorphosisin00Meri|page = 43 |language=nl}} ([[:s:nl:Pagina:Maria Sibylla Merian - Metamorphosis insectorum Surinamensium - Universiteitsbibliotheek Utrecht MAG AB 352 Rariora.pdf/45|plate 12]]).</ref>

At one time, to deal with the great diversity of cultivated bananas and plantains, botanists created many other names which are now regarded as synonyms of ''M.''&nbsp;×&nbsp;''paradisiaca'', such as ''M.&nbsp;corniculata'' Lour.,<ref name=Valm00/> used for a group of plantains with large fruit resembling the horns of a bull. Cultivated varieties are now given [[Cultivar#Cultivar names|cultivar names]], with the cultivars classified into groups and subgroups. Thus ''M.''&nbsp;×&nbsp;''paradisiaca'' is a species of banana as well as a cultivar.<ref name=Ploe07/> See [[list of banana cultivars]] for further information on the naming and classification of cultivars.

